 "figures": {
  "space": {
   "title": "What the rules of space make, with no brains",
   "caption": "Each column is one set of rules, each dot one world of about 1,500 agents after 300 iterations, the bar the median of ten. *parent*: a newborn is joined to its parent alone; *+2*: also to two of the parent's neighbours, drawn at random; *chain*: also, when an agent dies, the groups of its former neighbours that are not linked to one another are joined in a chain; *ring*: joined in a ring \u2014 B2's rules; *ring, 1* and *ring, 3*: the ring, with one or three of the parent's neighbours instead of two; *join all*: every former neighbour of the dead joined in a chain, linked or not; *ring, \u2264 8*: B2's rules with no agent above 8 links. The dashed lines are the health bar of Chapter 46. Cut shares and the best-connected agent's share are drawn on logarithmic axes, a cut of nothing at 0.0001.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** No runs: the worlds are made by the model itself, seeds 1 to 10 for every set of rules.\n\n**Data.** From the networks the model returns.\n\n1. A world of no brains (`research/oee/space.py`): it starts as a ring of 50 agents, each joined to its 6 nearest (Watts\u2013Strogatz, rewiring 0.2). Every iteration each agent has a child with probability 0.02 \u00d7 1,500 / *n*, plus 0.05 while there are fewer than 1,500 agents, and then dies with probability 0.02; after both, only the largest piece is kept. 300 iterations.\n2. Measure the last network (`space.measure`): the largest share of the others one agent's loss would cut off from the largest piece (`GraphOfLifeSimple.worst_node_cut_share`), the same for one link (`cutRisk`), the 2-core, links per agent, the ball dimension, and the best-connected agent's links over the agents less one.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py unbreakable`."
  },
